1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,262
Pump air into a bicycle tyre at one
valve, and the whole tube fills evenly.

2
00:00:05,262 --> 00:00:08,630
Four friends lift a heavy
bench more easily than one.

3
00:00:08,630 --> 00:00:11,017
A load shared is a load made light.

4
00:00:11,417 --> 00:00:13,133
Spreading out makes things easier.

5
00:00:13,133 --> 00:00:14,857
Electrons love to spread out too.

6
00:00:15,307 --> 00:00:18,788
Aarav is pumping his tyre and
thinking about last class.

7
00:00:18,788 --> 00:00:21,677
A tertiary carbocation
is the most stable one.

8
00:00:22,027 --> 00:00:22,927
But why?

9
00:00:22,712 --> 00:00:25,831
Its CH bonds are not even
touching the plus carbon.

10
00:00:25,831 --> 00:00:27,252
How can they help it?

11
00:00:27,752 --> 00:00:31,019
By the end, you will see two ways
electrons spread out, and why that makes

12
00:00:31,019 --> 00:00:31,919
molecules stable.

13
00:00:32,491 --> 00:00:34,054
First, recall resonance.

14
00:00:34,054 --> 00:00:38,110
When one Lewis drawing is not enough,
the real molecule is a hybrid of

15
00:00:38,110 --> 00:00:39,213
several drawings.

16
00:00:39,563 --> 00:00:42,815
For this, a molecule
needs a conjugated system.

17
00:00:42,815 --> 00:00:47,540
That means single and double bonds
taking turns, like a zebra crossing.

18
00:00:47,890 --> 00:00:50,673
A lone pair next to a
double bond also counts.

19
00:00:50,673 --> 00:00:53,395
Then electrons can flow
along the whole system.

20
00:00:53,745 --> 00:00:57,790
When a group pushes or pulls electrons
along this system, we call it the

21
00:00:57,790 --> 00:01:00,037
resonance effect, or mesomeric effect.

22
00:01:00,387 --> 00:01:02,601
Groups that push electrons in show plus R.

23
00:01:02,601 --> 00:01:04,893
Groups that pull electrons
out show minus R.

24
00:01:05,543 --> 00:01:07,918
Let's watch it on our molecule bench.

25
00:01:07,918 --> 00:01:09,101
Here is aniline.

26
00:01:09,101 --> 00:01:11,639
A benzene ring with an NH two group.

27
00:01:11,989 --> 00:01:15,957
The nitrogen has a lone pair, right
next to the ring's double bonds.

28
00:01:15,957 --> 00:01:17,453
Watch the curved arrows.

29
00:01:17,853 --> 00:01:22,340
The lone pair moves in to make a new
bond, and a ring pi pair moves onto

30
00:01:22,340 --> 00:01:23,240
a carbon.

31
00:01:23,111 --> 00:01:26,544
That carbon, next to the
group, is the ortho carbon.

32
00:01:26,544 --> 00:01:28,142
It gets a minus charge.

33
00:01:28,542 --> 00:01:32,711
Another shift, and the minus charge
moves to the opposite carbon, the para

34
00:01:32,711 --> 00:01:33,611
position.

35
00:01:33,380 --> 00:01:35,284
Then to the other ortho carbon.

36
00:01:35,684 --> 00:01:39,721
So in aniline, the ortho and para
carbons become richer in electrons.

37
00:01:39,721 --> 00:01:41,535
NH two is a plus R group.

38
00:01:41,935 --> 00:01:46,715
Plus R groups include halogens,
OH, OR, NH two, and the other amino

39
00:01:46,715 --> 00:01:47,615
groups.

40
00:01:47,758 --> 00:01:48,938
Now nitrobenzene.

41
00:01:48,938 --> 00:01:53,270
The nitro group pulls the ring's
electrons out, so ortho and para become

42
00:01:53,270 --> 00:01:55,097
poorer, with plus charges.

43
00:01:55,097 --> 00:02:00,377
Minus R groups include COOH, CHO,
the CO group, CN, and NO two.

44
00:02:01,027 --> 00:02:02,394
Now Aarav's question.

45
00:02:02,394 --> 00:02:04,812
How do CH bonds help a carbocation?

46
00:02:04,812 --> 00:02:05,844
Step by step.

47
00:02:06,194 --> 00:02:07,094
Step one.

48
00:02:07,011 --> 00:02:11,842
In the ethyl cation, the plus carbon
has an empty p orbital, an empty seat

49
00:02:11,842 --> 00:02:12,907
for electrons.

50
00:02:13,657 --> 00:02:14,557
Step two.

51
00:02:14,375 --> 00:02:16,727
The carbon next to it holds CH bonds.

52
00:02:16,727 --> 00:02:18,651
These are called alpha hydrogens.

53
00:02:19,401 --> 00:02:20,317
Step three.

54
00:02:20,317 --> 00:02:24,799
The electron pair of one CH bond
leans over and partly fills the empty

55
00:02:24,799 --> 00:02:25,699
seat.

56
00:02:25,356 --> 00:02:27,298
The plus charge gets shared.

57
00:02:28,048 --> 00:02:28,948
Step four.

58
00:02:28,739 --> 00:02:31,103
Sharing the charge makes
the ion more stable.

59
00:02:31,103 --> 00:02:34,101
This spreading of sigma electrons
is called hyperconjugation.

60
00:02:34,101 --> 00:02:36,061
It is also called no bond resonance.

61
00:02:36,811 --> 00:02:40,496
Hyperconjugation also works next
to a double bond, as in propene.

62
00:02:41,146 --> 00:02:42,764
Now count the helpers.

63
00:02:42,764 --> 00:02:45,114
Each alpha hydrogen is one helper.

64
00:02:45,464 --> 00:02:48,443
The methyl cation has
zero alpha hydrogens.

65
00:02:48,443 --> 00:02:50,450
The ethyl cation has three.

66
00:02:50,450 --> 00:02:52,562
The isopropyl cation has six.

67
00:02:52,562 --> 00:02:55,055
The tertiary butyl cation has nine.

68
00:02:55,455 --> 00:02:58,468
More helpers, more
spreading, more stability.

69
00:02:58,468 --> 00:03:02,061
That is why the tertiary
carbocation is the most stable.

70
00:03:02,711 --> 00:03:04,717
Compare the two effects side by side.

71
00:03:04,717 --> 00:03:07,002
Resonance moves pi
electrons and lone pairs.

72
00:03:07,002 --> 00:03:09,652
Hyperconjugation moves
sigma electrons of CH bonds.

73
00:03:10,002 --> 00:03:11,062
Pause and think.

74
00:03:11,062 --> 00:03:14,860
Which cation has more hyperconjugation,
the ethyl cation or the tertiary

75
00:03:14,860 --> 00:03:15,760
butyl cation?

76
00:03:18,938 --> 00:03:19,990
Tertiary butyl!

77
00:03:19,990 --> 00:03:23,803
It has nine alpha hydrogens,
the ethyl cation has only three.

78
00:03:24,453 --> 00:03:25,449
One last idea.

79
00:03:25,449 --> 00:03:27,892
Organic reactions come in four main types.

80
00:03:28,242 --> 00:03:30,930
Substitution swaps one group for another.

81
00:03:30,930 --> 00:03:33,112
Addition adds to a double bond.

82
00:03:33,112 --> 00:03:36,275
Elimination removes atoms
to make a double bond.

83
00:03:36,275 --> 00:03:39,629
Rearrangement shuffles the
atoms within one molecule.

84
00:03:40,279 --> 00:03:41,179
Remember this.

85
00:03:41,013 --> 00:03:42,762
Spread out electrons, stable molecule.

86
00:03:42,762 --> 00:03:45,835
Resonance spreads pi electrons,
hyperconjugation spreads sigma electrons.

87
00:03:46,535 --> 00:03:48,226
Now, one worked example.

88
00:03:48,526 --> 00:03:52,698
How many alpha hydrogens does the two
butyl cation have, and how does it

89
00:03:52,698 --> 00:03:54,743
compare with the isopropyl cation?

90
00:03:55,243 --> 00:03:57,388
The plus carbon is carbon two.

91
00:03:57,388 --> 00:04:02,155
Its neighbours are carbon one, a
methyl group, and carbon three, a CH

92
00:04:02,155 --> 00:04:03,055
two group.

93
00:04:03,790 --> 00:04:05,444
Carbon one has three hydrogens.

94
00:04:05,444 --> 00:04:07,098
Carbon three has two hydrogens.

95
00:04:07,098 --> 00:04:09,280
Three plus two gives five alpha hydrogens.

96
00:04:10,030 --> 00:04:14,449
The isopropyl cation has two methyl
groups next to the plus carbon.

97
00:04:14,449 --> 00:04:16,392
Three plus three gives six.

98
00:04:17,142 --> 00:04:20,059
Both are secondary carbocations,
but count each one carefully.

99
00:04:20,059 --> 00:04:21,741
Six beats five in hyperconjugation.

100
00:04:22,391 --> 00:04:23,853
Now, the exam trap.

101
00:04:24,203 --> 00:04:27,977
Some students count every hydrogen in
the cation, including hydrogens far

102
00:04:27,977 --> 00:04:30,109
away, or on the plus carbon itself.

103
00:04:30,509 --> 00:04:31,409
Wrong.

104
00:04:30,990 --> 00:04:34,764
Count only the hydrogens on the carbons
directly joined to the plus carbon.

105
00:04:34,764 --> 00:04:36,382
Those are the alpha hydrogens.

106
00:04:36,382 --> 00:04:38,616
Hydrogens on the plus carbon do not count.

107
00:04:39,266 --> 00:04:40,614
Where is this asked?

108
00:04:40,614 --> 00:04:45,073
Questions on plus R and minus R groups,
counting alpha hydrogens, and ordering

109
00:04:45,073 --> 00:04:46,403
carbocation stability.

110
00:04:47,003 --> 00:04:48,030
Exam level now.

111
00:04:48,030 --> 00:04:51,792
In which compound does resonance make
the ortho and para carbons richer

112
00:04:51,792 --> 00:04:52,692
in electrons?

113
00:04:52,680 --> 00:04:53,751
Pause and solve.

114
00:04:59,151 --> 00:05:00,880
The answer is C, aniline.

115
00:05:01,380 --> 00:05:04,249
NH two has a lone pair next to the ring.

116
00:05:04,249 --> 00:05:05,838
It pushes electrons in.

117
00:05:05,838 --> 00:05:06,738
Plus R.

118
00:05:07,036 --> 00:05:11,326
The nitro, CHO and CN groups all
pull electrons out of the ring.

119
00:05:11,326 --> 00:05:12,937
They are minus R groups.

120
00:05:13,437 --> 00:05:16,261
Only aniline makes its ortho and
para carbons richer in electrons.

121
00:05:16,861 --> 00:05:18,401
Here is your recap.

122
00:05:18,401 --> 00:05:19,984
Screenshot this card.

123
00:05:23,184 --> 00:05:25,370
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

124
00:05:25,770 --> 00:05:26,670
One.

125
00:05:26,262 --> 00:05:28,143
What is a conjugated system?

126
00:05:28,943 --> 00:05:29,843
Two.

127
00:05:29,455 --> 00:05:33,796
Why is NH two a plus R group,
but NO two a minus R group?

128
00:05:34,596 --> 00:05:35,496
Three.

129
00:05:35,104 --> 00:05:38,353
How many alpha hydrogens does
the tertiary butyl cation have?

130
00:05:39,253 --> 00:05:44,297
Next time: how do chemists pull one
pure compound out of a messy mixture?

131
00:05:44,297 --> 00:05:47,611
We find out in purification
of organic compounds.
